-- title:
--   Affine places of the Drinfeld curve are its k-points
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and let $k$ be an algebraically closed field equipped with an algebra structure over $\mathrm{GF}(q^2)$, and assume the coordinate ring `CoordRing q k`, the quotient of $k[x_0,x_1]$ by the ideal generated by `drinfeldPoly q k - 1`, is a domain; let $F =$ `drinfeldFunctionField q k` be its fraction field, and let $X, Y \in F$ be the images of the elements `x q k` and `y q k` of `CoordRing q k`. Here a place of $F$ over $k$ is a valuation subring of $F$ which contains the image of $k$, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring; the nonunits of such a subring form its maximal ideal. The assertion is the conjunction of two statements. First, for all $a, b \in k$ with $ab^q - a^q b = 1$ there is exactly one place $v$ of $F$ over $k$ with both $X - a$ and $Y - b$ in the maximal ideal of $v$. Second, for every place $v$ of $F$ over $k$ whose valuation subring contains both $X$ and $Y$, there exist $a, b \in k$ with $ab^q - a^q b = 1$ such that $X - a$ and $Y - b$ lie in the maximal ideal of $v$.
--
--   This is the census of the affine places of the function field of the Drinfeld curve $xy^q - x^qy = 1$: sending a place whose valuation ring contains $X$ and $Y$ to the pair of residues $(X(v), Y(v))$ is a bijection onto the set of $k$-points of the curve, each point being the centre of precisely one place. It underlies the subsequent analysis of the action of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{F}_q)$ on the places of this function field, in particular the identification of fixed points and of orbits of affine places.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_DrinfeldCurve_affinePlaces_census.lean

